# Agent Instructions

`{{ .name }}` is an Intropy job: a run-to-completion .NET 10 job with one
`IJob` to implement. Each run calls `Job.ExecuteAsync` once, then exits.

## Project overview

- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Component name: `{{ .name | kebabcase }}` (telemetry service name, the
  run's span name, and the prefix of its idempotency and incident records)
- Incident source: `urn:{{ .organization }}:{{ .name | kebabcase }}`
  (`Constants.IncidentSource`)
- Messaging and ports: none. The job publishes and subscribes to nothing.
- Exit codes: `0` success, nothing to do or cancelled; `1` the job threw or
  reported `Failed` > 0; `2` sidecar unavailable
- Config keys (`src/appsettings*.json`):
  - `PlatformServices:IdempotencyServiceAppId`: `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}`
  - `PlatformServices:BusinessIncidentServiceAppId`: `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}`

## Important files

- `src/Job.cs`: the job. Implement `ExecuteAsync`, which returns a
  `RunSummary` (processed / failed / skipped).
- `src/Composition/Composition.cs`: DI registrations. Register what the job
  takes in its constructor before `AddJob<Job>()`. `{{ .name }}.Composition`
  is both a namespace and a class, so nested namespaces call
  `Composition.Composition`.
- `src/Constants.cs`: component name, organization, incident source.
- `src/Program.cs`: entrypoint; runs the framework's `JobRunner`.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/`: the job's own logic, without a host.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/`: composition, and one run under the
  real `JobRunner` with the sidecar and platform clients faked (`CreateServices`
  in `RunnerTests.cs`).
- `Dockerfile`: image build (`docker build -t {{ .appId }} .`).

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test        # or task test:unit / task test:integration
task coverage    # Cobertura report under the integration project's TestResults/
```

## Development notes

- The work goes in `Job.cs` and the services it takes; leave `Program.cs` and
  the telemetry, Dapr and platform-client registrations as they are.
- One process is one job: a second `AddJob`, or a framework block on the same
  provider, fails at startup.
- A run must be safe to repeat: a failed run is re-run on the next schedule,
  and a cancelled run leaves its remaining work for the next one.
- A service that reaches outside the process (HTTP, a database, Dapr) is an
  edge: fake it in `CreateServices` like the platform clients.

## Framework guidance

Framework how-to (pipelines, blocks, endpoints, Dapr components, testing) lives in the Intropy documentation at <https://docs.intropy.io>. For CLI facts, read `intropy template show` and `intropy <command> --help` at the moment you need them; template releases change.
